# Full-depth felt-experience: non-beverage alcohol, sedating meds, plant drugs.
from gen import D
for k,p,i,t in [
("dimenhydrinate","plateau",1,"Some cry out or try to leave."),
("dimenhydrinate","comedown",1,"Guilt may follow."),
("anticholinergic-meds","peak",0,"Pupils stay wide."),
("anticholinergic-meds","comedown",1,"Anxiety may linger."),
]: D[k]["feel"][p][i]+=" "+t
F={}
F["alcohol-nonbeverage"]={
"onset":["Non-beverage alcohol means alcohol-containing products not meant to be drunk: mouthwash, hand sanitizer, rubbing alcohol (isopropyl), cooking extracts, cologne, and methylated or denatured spirits. They are usually drunk when beverage alcohol is unaffordable or unavailable. Ethanol-based products hit like strong liquor, with a burning throat, warm stomach and spreading flush; isopropyl causes a harsher burn, nausea and stomach pain.","Mentally ethanol products bring the familiar loosening of drunkenness, but often quickly and heavily because of their high strength. Isopropyl brings a heavier, more sedating intoxication, roughly twice as intoxicating as ethanol, with confusion and dizziness.","Many products contain other toxins. Methanol, found in some windshield fluid, fuel and illegally made spirits, can cause blindness and death, often after a delay of many hours in which the person seems only drunk. Mass methanol poisonings happen worldwide."],
"peak":["At the peak the body is clumsy and heavy, with slurred speech, poor balance, nausea and vomiting. Isopropyl causes stomach bleeding, a fruity smell on the breath, and low blood pressure. Methanol poisoning brings headache, abdominal pain and rapid breathing hours later.","Mentally there is heavy drunkenness: confusion, poor judgment, memory blackouts and mood swings. Methanol poisoning adds blurred or 'snowstorm' vision and confusion that worsens rather than improves.","Breathing can slow and stop, people can choke on vomit, and methanol or ethylene glycol cause severe acid buildup, blindness, kidney failure and death. Emergency treatment, including antidotes like fomepizole and dialysis, can save sight and life if started early."],
"plateau":["Because these products are so strong, people may stay heavily intoxicated for many hours, with repeated vomiting, falls and stupor. Sanitizer gels and mouthwashes can also irritate the stomach and throat.","Mentally the plateau is a fog of confusion, memory loss and fluctuating mood. Some people become withdrawn and drowsy, others agitated.","Any vision changes, severe headache, fast breathing or worsening confusion after drinking unknown alcohol or industrial products needs emergency care immediately, even if the person seemed fine at first."],
"comedown":["The comedown is a severe hangover: headache, nausea, vomiting, stomach pain, shakiness and dehydration. After isopropyl, stomach irritation and weakness can persist.","Mentally people feel foggy, low, anxious and ashamed, often with blackouts of the night before. Craving for more alcohol to relieve withdrawal is common in people with alcohol dependence.","People drinking non-beverage alcohol are often severely dependent and may face dangerous withdrawal, including seizures and delirium tremens, if they stop suddenly. Medically supported withdrawal, housing support and harm-reduction services save lives."]}
F["promethazine"]={
"onset":["Promethazine (Phenergan) is a sedating antihistamine prescribed for nausea, allergies and sleep, and it is the main ingredient besides codeine in cough syrups misused as 'lean', 'purple drank' or 'sizzurp'. Taken as directed, it brings drowsiness within about half an hour, a dry mouth, heavy eyelids and a relaxed, slowed body.","Mentally the onset is a heavy, cloudy calm, often with mild confusion and a dreamy detachment. Combined with codeine, many describe a warm, slow, syrupy euphoria; promethazine alone is mostly sedating.","Promethazine strengthens the breathing-slowing effects of opioids, alcohol, benzodiazepines and other sedatives. Lean mixtures have caused deaths, and naloxone can reverse the opioid component."],
"peak":["At the peak the body is very heavy and drowsy, coordination is poor, and speech may slow. The mouth is dry, vision may blur and some feel dizzy on standing because blood pressure drops.","Mentally there is deep sedation and slowed thinking. Larger amounts can cause confusion, restlessness, agitation or even hallucinations, as it has anticholinergic effects like diphenhydramine. Some feel strangely restless, an unpleasant feeling called akathisia.","Children under two must never be given promethazine because it can stop breathing. In adults, overdose can cause seizures, heart rhythm problems and coma."],
"plateau":["The plateau lasts several hours. The body stays heavy and sluggish, often drifting into a long sleep. In lean use, people may nod in and out, especially with codeine.","Mentally the mind stays slow and foggy, with poor memory and a dreamy, detached mood. Some feel low or flat as the sedation lingers.","Sleeping on the back while very sedated risks choking on vomit, and combinations with opioids or alcohol risk stopped breathing; anyone hard to wake should be placed on their side, given naloxone if opioids may be involved, and emergency help called."],
"comedown":["Waking from promethazine often comes with grogginess, dry mouth, headache and a heavy, slowed body that can last into the next day.","Mentally there can be fog, low motivation and mild irritability or anxiety.","Regular lean users can develop opioid dependence and dental problems from sugary syrups, and stopping can bring withdrawal symptoms. Treatment for opioid use disorder can help."]}
F["quetiapine"]={
"onset":["Quetiapine (Seroquel) is an antipsychotic used for schizophrenia, bipolar disorder and sometimes depression, and it is often prescribed off-label in low amounts for sleep. It is sometimes misused, especially in prisons, for its sedation. Taken, it brings heavy drowsiness within about an hour, a dry mouth, stuffy nose, and often dizziness on standing.","Mentally the onset is a heavy, blunting calm. Many describe thoughts slowing, worries fading and a strong pull toward sleep. Some feel a dreamy, detached state if they fight the sleepiness.","Quetiapine lowers blood pressure, which causes fainting and falls, and in overdose it can cause dangerously low blood pressure, fast heart rate, heart rhythm problems, seizures and coma, especially mixed with alcohol or other sedatives."],
"peak":["At the peak the body is very heavy, clumsy and sleepy. Standing may bring a rush of dizziness or fainting. The heart may race, the mouth is dry and constipation can begin.","Mentally the peak is deep sedation and mental slowing. Some feel calm and numb; others feel confused or restless, an inner jitteriness called akathisia. Larger amounts can cause confusion and delirium.","Very sedated people should be placed on their side and monitored, and emergency services called if they cannot be woken or their breathing seems abnormal."],
"plateau":["The plateau is long, often a full night's sleep, sometimes extending into the morning. The body stays heavy and slow, appetite may increase, and dreams can be vivid or absent.","Mentally the mind stays dulled and emotionally blunted, with little interest or motivation. Some people value this as relief from racing thoughts or anxiety.","Long-term use can cause weight gain, diabetes, high cholesterol and movement disorders, which is why low-dose sleep use is debated."],
"comedown":["Waking often brings a heavy 'hangover', with grogginess, dry mouth and slowed movement into the morning.","Mentally people may feel foggy, flat and slow to get going.","Stopping suddenly after regular use can cause insomnia, nausea, sweating, irritability and, in people with bipolar disorder or psychosis, relapse. Tapering with a doctor is important."]}
F["antidepressants-misused"]={
"onset":["Some antidepressants are occasionally misused, most notably bupropion (Wellbutrin), which is sometimes crushed and snorted for a stimulant effect, and the older tricyclics and trazodone or mirtazapine for sedation. At prescribed doses most antidepressants produce no high at all. Misused bupropion can bring a mild, jittery, stimulant buzz with a fast heart and dry mouth.","Mentally misused bupropion may give a short, anxious alertness rather than real euphoria. Sedating antidepressants mostly bring heavy drowsiness and grogginess, with a dull, dreamy calm.","Bupropion in large amounts can cause seizures, sometimes many hours later, along with heart rhythm problems. Tricyclic antidepressant overdose is one of the most dangerous medicine overdoses, causing seizures, heart rhythm problems and death."],
"peak":["At the peak with bupropion, the body may feel tense, jittery and hot, with a racing heart, tremor and nausea. With tricyclics or mirtazapine, the body is heavy, sleepy and dry-mouthed, with blurred vision and constipation.","Mentally there is restlessness and anxiety with bupropion, or heavy sedation and confusion with sedating antidepressants. Large amounts of tricyclics cause delirium and hallucinations from anticholinergic effects.","Seizures, heart rhythm problems and serotonin syndrome (especially when mixed with other serotonin drugs) need emergency care."],
"plateau":["Effects can last many hours. Bupropion misuse keeps the body wired and uncomfortable; sedating antidepressants keep a person heavily drowsy with dizziness on standing.","Mentally there is lingering anxiety or a heavy, dull fog, and sometimes confusion.","Overdose symptoms may be delayed, so anyone who has taken large amounts should be checked in hospital even if they feel fine."],
"comedown":["The comedown brings tiredness, nausea, headache and sometimes shakiness.","Mentally mood may dip, with anxiety, irritability or flatness.","Stopping prescribed antidepressants suddenly can cause discontinuation symptoms, including dizziness, 'brain zaps', irritability and flu-like feelings. Changes should be made with a doctor."]}
F["kava"]={
"onset":["Kava is a drink made from the root of a Pacific Island plant, traditionally shared in ceremonies and social gatherings in Fiji, Vanuatu, Tonga and Samoa. Its active compounds, kavalactones, come on within half an hour. The first sensation is a tingling numbness of the tongue and lips from the earthy, bitter drink, followed by a warm, loose relaxation in the muscles.","Mentally the onset is a calm, clear-headed sociability. Many describe it as quieting anxiety without clouding thought, with a gentle contentment and openness to conversation.","Kava is generally considered low risk in traditional use, but heavy use, poor-quality products made from stems and leaves, and mixing with alcohol have been linked to liver injury."],
"peak":["At the peak the body feels relaxed and heavy, especially the legs, with mild numbness and sometimes a slight unsteadiness. Some feel nauseous from the taste or volume of drink.","Mentally there is calm, warmth, contentment and an easy, unhurried sociability. Larger amounts bring drowsiness and a dreamy detachment, and people often fall asleep.","Coordination is impaired, and driving after heavy kava drinking is unsafe."],
"plateau":["The plateau lasts a couple of hours. The body stays relaxed and sleepy, and some people's vision becomes sensitive to light.","Mentally people stay calm and quiet, often enjoying conversation or music, and gradually become sleepy.","Heavy daily drinkers can develop dry, scaly skin (kava dermopathy), weight loss and red eyes, which resolve when use is reduced."],
"comedown":["Most people wake after kava sessions without a hangover, though heavy drinking can leave them sluggish and groggy.","Mentally many feel rested and calm.","People with liver disease, or taking medicines that affect the liver, should avoid kava. Yellowing skin or eyes, dark urine or abdominal pain need medical care."]}
F["nutmeg"]={
"onset":["Nutmeg is a common kitchen spice that, in large amounts, contains myristicin and related compounds that cause intoxication. Its onset is very slow, often several hours, so many people believe it hasn't worked. The first signs are a dry mouth, flushing, a fast heart and nausea, sometimes with abdominal pain.","Mentally the onset is a vague, heavy unreality, with confusion and a dreamlike mood creeping in. Many people describe it as unpleasant rather than euphoric.","Because effects take so long to begin, people often take more, leading to severe poisoning. Most experiences reported to poison centers are uncomfortable and frightening."],
"peak":["At the peak the body feels heavy, dizzy and sick, with a fast heart, flushed face, dry mouth and sometimes vomiting. Walking is unsteady.","Mentally there can be confusion, distorted perception, anxiety, agitation and, in higher amounts, hallucinations and a sense of impending doom.","Severe poisoning can cause heart rhythm problems, low blood pressure, seizures and, very rarely, death, especially in combination with other drugs."],
"plateau":["The plateau is very long, often lasting a day or more. The body remains drowsy, nauseated, dry and heavy.","Mentally confusion, fogginess and anxiety persist, and many people sleep a long time.","Poison control can advise on symptoms."],
"comedown":["The comedown can last days, with exhaustion, headache, dry mouth and body aches.","Mentally there may be confusion, low mood and a strong wish never to repeat it.","Large amounts are dangerous, especially for pregnant people and those with heart conditions."]}
F["amanita-muscaria"]={
"onset":["Amanita muscaria is the classic red-capped, white-spotted mushroom of fairy tales. Its active chemicals, ibotenic acid and muscimol, act on GABA and glutamate receptors rather than serotonin, so it is not a psilocybin mushroom. Effects begin slowly, over an hour or two, with nausea, salivation, sweating and a heavy, dizzy drowsiness.","Mentally the onset is a dreamy, foggy sedation, sometimes with odd euphoria, confusion or a sense of drifting between waking and sleep. Some people feel restless and twitchy before sleep comes.","Amanita muscaria is easily confused with deadly mushrooms, especially other Amanita species. Amanita products, sold as gummies in US shops since around 2023, have caused poisonings, and potency varies greatly."],
"peak":["At the peak the body may be heavy and floppy or twitchy, with muscle jerks, unsteady walking, sweating, drooling and sometimes vomiting.","Mentally there may be a dream-like delirium, with distorted size and shape of objects, confusion, euphoria or agitation, and drifting in and out of sleep with vivid dreams. Memory is often patchy.","Severe poisoning causes seizures, deep sedation, coma and agitation, and needs emergency care."],
"plateau":["The plateau lasts several hours, often spent in a deep, dream-filled sleep. Muscle twitches and nausea may continue.","Mentally confusion and dreaminess persist.","Children who eat these mushrooms or gummies need urgent care."],
"comedown":["The comedown brings tiredness, headache and sometimes nausea.","Mentally there may be fogginess and confusion.","Never eat wild mushrooms unless identified by an expert."]}
F["salvia"]={
"onset":["Salvia divinorum is a Mexican sage traditionally used by Mazatec healers. Its active compound, salvinorin A, acts on kappa-opioid receptors. Smoked or vaped, it takes effect within seconds: a pressure or pulling sensation in the body, as if gravity has shifted, tingling and a sudden heaviness.","Mentally the onset is an abrupt loss of normal reality. People often describe the world folding, peeling or turning like pages, being pulled into a different place, or merging with objects. Laughter or fear can erupt suddenly.","Because people often lose awareness of their surroundings, they may stand, walk, or fall. A sober sitter and a safe, clear space matter."],
"peak":["At the peak, which lasts only a few minutes, the body may feel absent, twisted or stretched, with loss of coordination and sometimes involuntary movement.","Mentally the peak can be one of the most intense altered states: complete detachment from ordinary reality, becoming part of a pattern or object, meeting entities or living another life. Many find it frightening or overwhelming, not pleasurable.","Physical toxicity is low, but accidents and panic are the main risks."],
"plateau":["The plateau is short, followed by a gradual return over several minutes. The body slowly reconnects, often with a tingling, heavy feeling.","Mentally people may feel confused, unsure of what is real, laughing or emotional as the ordinary world reassembles.","Most people do not want to repeat it often."],
"comedown":["The comedown is quick, usually within half an hour, with mild headache or tiredness.","Mentally there can be a lingering sense of strangeness or reflection.","People with a history of psychosis should avoid salvia."]}
F["betel"]={
"onset":["Betel quid is a preparation of areca nut, often wrapped in betel leaf, chewed by hundreds of millions of people across South and Southeast Asia and the Pacific. Its main stimulant, arecoline, acts within minutes: a warm flush across the face and body, sweating, a slight rise in heart rate and a red-staining, bitter saliva.","Mentally the onset is a mild alertness and well-being, similar to a strong cup of coffee or tobacco, with improved focus and a sense of social comfort.","Areca nut is classified as a cause of cancer, especially mouth cancer, and many products are mixed with tobacco, which increases harm."],
"peak":["At the peak the body is warm and alert, with flushing, sweating, extra saliva and mild heart-rate increase.","Mentally there is mild euphoria, alertness and calm.","Heavy use can cause gum disease, staining, and oral submucous fibrosis, a stiffening of the mouth that limits opening and can become cancer."],
"plateau":["The plateau lasts as long as chewing continues. The body stays warm and slightly stimulated.","Mentally a mild, steady alertness continues.","Many people chew throughout the day."],
"comedown":["As effects fade, people may feel slightly tired.","Mentally a mild craving may appear.","Regular use causes dependence; quitting support helps."]}
F["blue-lotus-kanna"]={
"onset":["Blue lotus (Nymphaea caerulea), a water lily depicted in ancient Egyptian art, and kanna (Sceletium tortuosum), a South African succulent traditionally chewed by Khoisan peoples, are sold as 'legal' relaxants. Blue lotus is drunk as tea or smoked, bringing a gentle warmth and relaxation within half an hour. Kanna, chewed, snuffed or swallowed, can bring a light, warm mood lift and slight numbness of the mouth.","Mentally blue lotus brings a mild, dreamy calm and sometimes vivid dreams; kanna brings a soft, mood-brightening ease and sociability, a little like a mild empathogen.","Kanna acts on serotonin and should never be combined with antidepressants or other serotonin drugs. Product strength varies greatly."],
"peak":["At the peak the body feels relaxed and warm with mild sedation (blue lotus) or gentle stimulation (kanna).","Mentally there is calm, contentment and gentle euphoria.","Both are poorly studied, and serious effects are possible with high amounts or mixing."],
"plateau":["The plateau lasts a couple of hours.","Mentally the calm or uplift persists.","Driving may be impaired."],
"comedown":["The comedown is usually gentle, with mild tiredness.","Mentally people often feel rested.","Avoid combining with alcohol or medicines without advice."]}
for k,v in F.items():
    if k in D: D[k].setdefault("feel",{}).update(v)
